# The preset policy in kgsm-base decides whether this daemon runs on a node; applying it is symlink
# manipulation on disk, so it works during the transaction and in a chroot alike. Starting it is the
# node's post-transaction hook's job, which is where a running system is available.
#
# post_install only. An administrator who enabled this unit keeps it enabled across every later
# version — re-presetting on upgrade would quietly switch it back off.

post_install() {
    systemctl preset kgsm-dns.service > /dev/null 2>&1 || true

    cat <<'MSG'

  kgsm-dns is installed and switched OFF. A cluster publishes its names from one place, so which
  machine holds that capability is a decision rather than a default — installing the package claims
  nothing.

  To make this machine the DNS anchor:

      systemctl enable --now kgsm-dns.service

  Three things it needs from you, in /etc/kgsm-dns/kgsm-dns.env: the zone, the provider's id for
  that zone, and an API token for it. The token is held here and nowhere else, which is what lets
  every other member ask this one for a name instead of carrying a credential of its own.

  A machine joining a cluster also needs the shared secret in /etc/kgsm/kgsm-cluster.env.

  Until a zone and a token are set, this anchor holds the capability, says it is unconfigured, and
  publishes nothing.

MSG
}

post_upgrade() {
    cat <<'MSG'

  If pacman wrote a .pacnew beside /etc/kgsm-dns/kgsm-dns.env, merge it — new settings are declared
  there and a key the file never declares binds to nothing.

MSG
}
